<GuideLayout slug="reduce-discord-ram-usage">
  <p>
    Open Task Manager mid-game and you'll often find Discord sitting on more memory than
    the game you're playing. On a lot of Windows machines the stock client parks well over
    <strong>1 GB of RAM</strong> just to show a chat window. Here's why that happens, and
    seven things you can actually do about it.
  </p>

  <h2>Why Discord uses so much RAM</h2>
  <p>
    Discord is built on Electron — essentially a full copy of the Chrome browser bundled
    with the app. That means it runs multiple Chromium processes (the main app, a GPU
    process, renderers, utility helpers), each with its own memory footprint, before you've
    read a single message. On top of that browser baseline, a few things inflate it further:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hardware acceleration</strong> spins up a GPU process that can leak memory over long sessions.</li>
    <li><strong>Animated avatars, emojis, and stickers</strong> are decoded and held in memory as you scroll.</li>
    <li><strong>Lots of large servers</strong> mean more cached members, channels, and media.</li>
    <li><strong>Background telemetry and experiments</strong> add their own overhead you never see.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Seven ways to cut Discord's memory use</h2>

  <h3>1. Toggle hardware acceleration</h3>
  <p>
    Settings → Advanced → Hardware Acceleration. If you're CPU-bound, leaving it on offloads
    work to the GPU; if you're GPU-bound in a game, turning it off frees VRAM and can stop a
    slow memory creep. Test both — the right answer depends on your rig.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Kill the "reduced motion" and animation load</h3>
  <p>
    Settings → Accessibility → enable <strong>Reduced Motion</strong> and disable animated
    emoji and stickers. Fewer things decoding and looping in memory.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Close servers you don't use</h3>
  <p>
    Every server you're in is state Discord keeps warm. Leaving dead servers is the simplest
    way to shrink the cached member and channel lists.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Clear the cache</h3>
  <p>
    Quit Discord fully, then delete the contents of <code>%AppData%\discord\Cache</code> and
    <code>Code Cache</code>. A bloated cache doesn't just cost disk — it costs startup memory.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Actually quit it (don't just minimize)</h3>
  <p>
    Discord minimizes to the tray and keeps running. If you're not in a call, right-click the
    tray icon → Quit. Re-launching takes two seconds and resets the memory baseline.
  </p>

  <h3>6. Disable startup + run only when needed</h3>
  <p>
    Settings → Windows Settings → turn off "Open Discord" on startup. No reason for a 1 GB
    process to load before you've even opened a game.
  </p>

  <h3>7. Use a lighter-weight client</h3>
  <p>
    The structural fixes above help, but you're still running a full browser to read text.
    The biggest single win is swapping the client itself. A trimmed build strips the parts of
    Discord that pretend to be a browser — telemetry, crash beacons, ad-tech, growth
    experiments — and idles dramatically lower. On the same machine where stock Discord uses
    <strong>1,041 MB</strong>, our build sits at <strong>288 MB</strong>: same servers, same
    friends, same pings, ~753 MB lighter.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    Quick wins: quit-don't-minimize, clear cache, and leave dead servers. Bigger win: a client
    that wasn't built to spy on you.
  </blockquote>
</GuideLayout>
